// =============================================================================
// Aurora Preference — DAX measures
// =============================================================================
// Create a blank table called _Measures (Home > Enter data, one dummy column,
// delete the column after) and hang everything below off it, so the measures sit
// in one place rather than scattered across the fact tables.
//
// Everything is written against the model in powerbi/README.md §3. Relationships
// assumed: People[EmployeeID] 1--* Preferences, Responses, Alignments,
// WhatIfAssignment; DimRole[RoleKey] 1--* Preferences[RoleKey] and
// WhatIfAssignment[AssignedRoleKey] (the second inactive, see below).
// =============================================================================


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PAGE 1 — headline cards
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Total Colleagues = DISTINCTCOUNT ( People[EmployeeID] )

Total Areas = DISTINCTCOUNT ( People[Area] )

Total Teams = DISTINCTCOUNT ( People[Team] )

// The brief's "line managers G6/G7". IsLineManager is set in Power Query so the
// grade strings are trimmed and cased consistently first.
Total Line Managers =
CALCULATE ( [Total Colleagues], People[IsLineManager] = TRUE () )

// Roles the app offers, not rows in the capacity sheet — three of those have no
// key and cannot be matched to anything a person ranked.
Total Roles Available =
CALCULATE ( DISTINCTCOUNT ( DimRole[RoleKey] ), DimRole[InApp] = TRUE () )

Total Posts = SUM ( DimRole[Posts] )

// Every ranked row across everyone — the denominator for "interest".
Total Preferences Cast = COUNTROWS ( Preferences )

Respondents =
CALCULATE ( DISTINCTCOUNT ( Preferences[EmployeeID] ), Preferences[Rank] > 0 )

Completed Respondents =
CALCULATE (
    DISTINCTCOUNT ( Responses[EmployeeID] ),
    Responses[Stage2Status] = "Submitted"
)

// --- scope -------------------------------------------------------------------
// People holds everyone across the affected teams. The process covers only those
// with options -- roughly 72 of 104. Every rate below divides by the smaller
// number, because dividing by the larger one answers a question nobody asked.
Colleagues In Scope =
CALCULATE ( [Total Colleagues], People[HasOptions] = TRUE () )

// Page 1 carries a page-level filter on People[HasOptions], so that everything
// on it describes the 72 rather than the 104. These two have to survive that
// filter to keep saying what they mean -- without ALL, the context card would
// read 72 and the out-of-scope count would read 0, and the page would lose the
// only thing that explains why two different headcounts exist.
People On The System =
CALCULATE ( [Total Colleagues], ALL ( People[HasOptions] ) )

Colleagues Not In Scope = [People On The System] - [Colleagues In Scope]

Completion Rate =
DIVIDE ( [Completed Respondents], [Colleagues In Scope] )

Outstanding = [Colleagues In Scope] - [Completed Respondents]

// Headline pressure number, and the one the scope correction moves furthest.
// Above 1 means more people than posts. Against everyone on the system it reads
// about 1.35 -- oversubscribed, some people miss out. Against the people
// actually being moved it reads below 1 -- more posts than candidates, and the
// question becomes which posts go unfilled. Same data, opposite conclusion, and
// the second one is the true one.
// Both sides slicer-aware. Against [Total Posts] it silently mixed a filtered
// numerator with an unfiltered denominator: under a Grade slicer it divided the
// people OF THAT GRADE by EVERY post in the process, understating demand by a
// factor of the whole role list. See BUILD.md 9.5 and 9.9.
People Per Post = DIVIDE ( [Colleagues In Scope], [Posts Offered To Selection] )

// The same against posts the model can actually allocate -- Total Posts includes
// the three unkeyed capacity rows and R16, which no one can ever be given.
People Per Assignable Post = DIVIDE ( [Colleagues In Scope], [Assignable Posts] )


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PAGE 2 — respondent table (PAB-6119)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Stage 2 Status is a CALCULATED COLUMN ON People, not a measure. It is in
// BUILD.md section 7 with the other calculated columns, not here.
//
// It was a measure first, and that was wrong in a way worth recording because
// the failure is silent and general. A table visual builds its rows by
// crossjoining the distinct values of the columns on it, then drops the rows
// where every measure is blank -- that blank-removal is what prunes the
// combinations the relationships would exclude. The measure returned
// "Not started" rather than BLANK for a person with no answers, so nothing was
// ever blank, nothing was pruned, and the respondent table returned the full
// cartesian product of People against PreferenceWide: every person against
// every first-choice role name, ~2,000 rows.
//
// The general rule: A MEASURE THAT NEVER RETURNS BLANK WILL CARTESIAN-PRODUCT
// ANY TABLE VISUAL CARRYING COLUMNS FROM MORE THAN ONE TABLE. Where a value is
// wanted for every row of a dimension -- a status, a band, a label -- put it in
// a calculated column on that dimension, where it takes part in row generation
// instead of fighting it.
//
// As a column it can also drive a slicer, which a measure cannot.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PAGE 3 — demand, over/under subscription
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Applications = COUNTROWS ( Preferences )

First Choices  = CALCULATE ( [Applications], Preferences[Rank] = 1 )
Second Choices = CALCULATE ( [Applications], Preferences[Rank] = 2 )
Third Choices  = CALCULATE ( [Applications], Preferences[Rank] = 3 )

// Anyone who put this role anywhere in their top three.
Top 3 Interest = CALCULATE ( [Applications], Preferences[Rank] <= 3 )

Posts For Role = SUM ( DimRole[Posts] )

// --- what is open to the people currently selected ---------------------------
// Total Posts and Total Roles Available sum DimRole, and NOTHING on People
// filters DimRole -- filters run from the one side to the many, so People
// reaches Preferences and Eligibility and stops there. Slice by Grade and those
// two measures keep reporting 80 and 65: the whole estate, wearing the label of
// whatever was selected. Wrong in the worst way, because it looks answered.
//
// These three go through Eligibility, which People DOES filter, so they mean
// "offered to the people currently selected" and move when the slicer moves.
// Use them on any page with a People slicer on it.
Colleagues Offered Roles = DISTINCTCOUNT ( Eligibility[EmployeeID] )

Roles Offered To Selection = DISTINCTCOUNT ( Eligibility[RoleKey] )

// TREATAS pushes the eligible role keys back onto DimRole as a filter, which the
// relationship cannot do on its own. Without it this returns 80 every time.
Posts Offered To Selection =
CALCULATE (
    SUM ( DimRole[Posts] ),
    TREATAS ( VALUES ( Eligibility[RoleKey] ), DimRole[RoleKey] )
)

// How many people were even offered this role. A role with no interest may be
// unwanted, or may simply have been offered to four people -- and the heatmap
// cannot tell those apart without this. Needs DimRole[RoleKey] 1:* to
// Eligibility[RoleKey].
People Eligible For Role = DISTINCTCOUNT ( Eligibility[EmployeeID] )

// Demand against the population that could have expressed it, rather than
// against posts. Near 1 means almost everyone offered it wanted it.
Take Up Rate = DIVIDE ( [First Choices], [People Eligible For Role] )

// --- committed demand vs current intent -------------------------------------
// Preferences[Stage1Status] holds Draft AND Submitted. The app writes rows as
// people rank, not when they submit, so Applications above counts rankings that
// are still being edited. That is not wrong — with Phase 1 open it may be the
// only signal there is — but it is not the same question as "what have people
// committed to", and a heatmap that does not say which it is showing invites
// somebody to plan against numbers that are still moving.
//
// Both are kept. Page 3 shows them side by side rather than choosing.
Submitted Applications =
CALCULATE ( [Applications], Preferences[Stage1Status] = "Submitted" )

Draft Applications =
CALCULATE ( [Applications], Preferences[Stage1Status] = "Draft" )

Submitted First Choices =
CALCULATE ( [First Choices], Preferences[Stage1Status] = "Submitted" )

Submitted Respondents =
CALCULATE (
    DISTINCTCOUNT ( Preferences[EmployeeID] ),
    Preferences[Stage1Status] = "Submitted"
)

// Committed demand against posts — the same shape as Subscription Ratio but
// counting only rankings somebody has actually stood behind.
Submitted Subscription Ratio =
DIVIDE ( [Submitted First Choices], [Posts For Role] )

// The "is this settled yet?" number, and the one to read first on page 3.
// High means the heatmap is describing intentions people can still change.
Pct Demand Still Draft =
DIVIDE ( [Draft Applications], [Applications] )


// The heatmap value. Deliberately based on FIRST choices: second and third
// choices are consolation, and counting them flatters a role nobody actually
// wants most. Swap to [Top 3 Interest] for the softer view.
Subscription Ratio =
VAR Demand = [First Choices]
VAR Supply = [Posts For Role]
RETURN DIVIDE ( Demand, Supply )

// Signed pressure: +3 means three more first-choice takers than posts.
Oversubscription =
[First Choices] - [Posts For Role]

// Zero-post roles would divide by zero and read as infinitely oversubscribed,
// which is not useful — they are called out separately instead.
Subscription Band =
VAR Supply = [Posts For Role]
VAR Demand = [First Choices]
VAR Ratio  = DIVIDE ( Demand, Supply )
RETURN
    SWITCH (
        TRUE (),
        Supply = 0,      "No posts available",
        Demand = 0,      "Nobody chose it",
        Ratio < 1,       "Undersubscribed",
        Ratio = 1,       "Exactly filled",
        Ratio <= 2,      "Oversubscribed",
        "Heavily oversubscribed"
    )

Roles Oversubscribed =
COUNTROWS (
    FILTER (
        VALUES ( DimRole[RoleKey] ),
        [First Choices] > [Posts For Role] && [Posts For Role] > 0
    )
)

Roles With No Interest =
COUNTROWS (
    FILTER ( VALUES ( DimRole[RoleKey] ), [Top 3 Interest] = 0 )
)

// Data-quality counters. These belong on the page, not in a footnote — a
// dashboard that hides a broken join is worse than no dashboard.
Roles Missing A Key =
CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( DimRole ), DimRole[JoinStatus] = "Capacity sheet only - no role key" )

// In the capacity sheet but not in the app: nobody could have ranked it.
Roles Missing From The App =
CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( DimRole ), DimRole[JoinStatus] = "Capacity sheet only - missing from the app" )

// In the app but with no post count: people can rank it, but the model has no
// capacity to allocate and it reads as infinitely oversubscribed.
Roles Missing A Post Count =
CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( DimRole ), DimRole[JoinStatus] = "App only - no post count" )

Roles Not Reconciled =
CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( DimRole ), DimRole[JoinStatus] <> "Matched" )

Roles With Zero Posts =
CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( DimRole ), DimRole[Posts] = 0, DimRole[InCapacitySheet] = TRUE () )

// Keys people ranked that do not exist in the capacity sheet, and vice versa.
// Ranked rows whose RoleKey is in neither source. Should be zero; if it is not,
// somebody ranked a role that has since been deleted.
Preferences For Unknown Role =
CALCULATE (
    COUNTROWS ( Preferences ),
    FILTER ( Preferences, ISBLANK ( RELATED ( DimRole[RoleName] ) ) )
)


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PAGE 3 — what-if
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Modelled People = COUNTROWS ( WhatIfAssignment )

// --- Band 1: got a role they argued for -------------------------------------
Got 1st Choice = CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( WhatIfAssignment ), WhatIfAssignment[OutcomeRank] = 1 )
Got 2nd Choice = CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( WhatIfAssignment ), WhatIfAssignment[OutcomeRank] = 2 )
Got 3rd Choice = CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( WhatIfAssignment ), WhatIfAssignment[OutcomeRank] = 3 )

Got Justified Choice =
CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( WhatIfAssignment ), WhatIfAssignment[OutcomeBand] = "Justified choice" )

// --- Band 2: placed, but below the line they wrote a case for ---------------
// The number that did not exist before the two-pass rewrite, and the one that
// sizes the work: each of these is a conversation, not a crisis.
Placed Below Justification =
CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( WhatIfAssignment ), WhatIfAssignment[OutcomeBand] = "Below the justification line" )

// --- Band 3: nowhere to go --------------------------------------------------
// Every role this person ranked is full. Not a conversation — a problem.
Unplaceable =
CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( WhatIfAssignment ), WhatIfAssignment[OutcomeBand] = "Unplaceable" )

Pct Got 1st Choice = DIVIDE ( [Got 1st Choice], [Modelled People] )
Pct Got 2nd Choice = DIVIDE ( [Got 2nd Choice], [Modelled People] )
Pct Got 3rd Choice = DIVIDE ( [Got 3rd Choice], [Modelled People] )

Pct Got Justified Choice       = DIVIDE ( [Got Justified Choice],       [Modelled People] )
Pct Placed Below Justification = DIVIDE ( [Placed Below Justification], [Modelled People] )
Pct Unplaceable                = DIVIDE ( [Unplaceable],                [Modelled People] )

// How far down someone had to go, among those who fell below the line. Reads
// "on average the 6th choice" — the higher this is, the further the fallback
// is from anything the person actually made a case for.
Average Fallback Rank =
CALCULATE (
    AVERAGE ( WhatIfAssignment[OutcomeRank] ),
    WhatIfAssignment[OutcomeBand] = "Below the justification line"
)

// --- Posts ------------------------------------------------------------------
Posts Filled =
CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( WhatIfAssignment ), NOT ISBLANK ( WhatIfAssignment[AssignedRoleKey] ) )

// NOT [Total Posts]. Total Posts sums every row in DimRole, including the three
// unkeyed capacity rows and any role the app does not offer — posts the model
// could never fill. Subtracting from it would have shown 4+ posts permanently
// unfilled and sent the reader hunting for a bug that is really a data problem.
// Page 6 is where those belong.
Assignable Posts =
CALCULATE (
    SUM ( DimRole[Posts] ),
    DimRole[JoinStatus] IN { "Matched", "Matched - but zero posts" }
)

Posts Unfilled = [Assignable Posts] - [Posts Filled]

// Put this on the page next to the percentages. One run is one shuffle; the
// numbers move when the seed moves, and the reader needs to know that.
What If Caveat =
"One random run at seed " & SELECTEDVALUE ( WhatIfSeedValue[Seed], 1 )
    & ". Allocated in two passes: everyone competes for the top three they wrote"
    & " a justification for, then anyone still unplaced falls back to the roles"
    & " they ranked but did not argue for. Order decides who wins a contested"
    & " role, so treat the three bands as a rough shape and no individual row as"
    & " a decision."


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PAGE 4/5 — alignment, accept vs challenge
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Alignments Published = COUNTROWS ( Alignments )

Decisions Made =
CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( Alignments ), Alignments[Status] = "Submitted" )

Accepted =
CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( Alignments ), Alignments[Decision] = "Accepted", Alignments[Status] = "Submitted" )

Challenged =
CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( Alignments ), Alignments[Decision] = "Rejected", Alignments[Status] = "Submitted" )

Awaiting Decision = [Alignments Published] - [Decisions Made]

Acceptance Rate = DIVIDE ( [Accepted], [Decisions Made] )
Challenge Rate  = DIVIDE ( [Challenged], [Decisions Made] )

// How many people did NOT get one of their three, among those told their role.
// This is the number most likely to predict a challenge.
Aligned Outside Top 3 =
CALCULATE (
    COUNTROWS ( Alignments ),
    FILTER (
        Alignments,
        VAR k = Alignments[AssignedRoleKey]
        VAR e = Alignments[EmployeeID]
        RETURN
            NOT ISBLANK ( k )
                && COUNTROWS (
                    FILTER ( Preferences, Preferences[EmployeeID] = e && Preferences[RoleKey] = k && Preferences[Rank] <= 3 )
                ) = 0
    )
)

// The app writes "Accepted" / "Rejected" (Phase2-alignment-formulas.powerfx).
// Comparing against "Accept" / "Reject" returns zero with no error.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PAGE 5b — allocation outcomes: which preference did people actually get
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The business asked to report how many people were matched to one of their
// preferred options. These read Alignments[AchievedRank], which is the rank the
// assigned role held in that person's OWN list.
//
// The point of the page is the pair: page 4 PREDICTS a distribution from an
// unallocated cohort, this REPORTS the one that happened. Put them side by side
// and the prediction can be judged rather than believed.

Assigned People =
CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( Alignments ), NOT ISBLANK ( Alignments[AssignedRoleKey] ) )

Achieved Top 3 =
CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( Alignments ), Alignments[AchievedBand] = "Top 3 - a choice they justified" )

Achieved Below Top 3 =
CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( Alignments ), Alignments[AchievedBand] = "Ranked, below the top 3" )

// Given a role they never ranked at all. The number most likely to be
// challenged, and the one the whole exercise is trying to keep small.
Achieved Outside Ranking =
CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( Alignments ), Alignments[AchievedBand] = "Not one they ranked" )

Pct Achieved Top 3           = DIVIDE ( [Achieved Top 3],           [Assigned People] )
Pct Achieved Below Top 3     = DIVIDE ( [Achieved Below Top 3],     [Assigned People] )
Pct Achieved Outside Ranking = DIVIDE ( [Achieved Outside Ranking], [Assigned People] )

// "Matched to one of their preferred options" as the business phrased it --
// anywhere in their own list, justified or not.
Pct Matched To A Preference =
DIVIDE ( [Achieved Top 3] + [Achieved Below Top 3], [Assigned People] )

// Median rather than mean: one person placed at their 11th choice would drag an
// average and misdescribe everyone else.
Median Achieved Rank = MEDIAN ( Alignments[AchievedRank] )

Awaiting Allocation = [Colleagues In Scope] - [Assigned People]


// Tick-box analysis, off the unpivoted table.
Reason Mentions = COUNTROWS ( RejectReasonsUnpivoted )

Pct Of Challenges Citing Reason =
DIVIDE ( DISTINCTCOUNT ( RejectReasonsUnpivoted[EmployeeID] ), [Challenged] )

Reasons Per Challenge =
DIVIDE ( [Reason Mentions], DISTINCTCOUNT ( RejectReasonsUnpivoted[EmployeeID] ) )


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Free text
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Answers Written =
CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( Responses ), NOT ISBLANK ( Responses[ResponseText] ) )

Median Answer Length = MEDIAN ( Responses[WordCount] )

Answers At The Limit =
CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( Responses ), Responses[WordCount] >= 145 )

People Mentioning Theme = DISTINCTCOUNT ( ResponseThemes[EmployeeID] )

Pct Mentioning Theme =
DIVIDE ( [People Mentioning Theme], [Completed Respondents] )

// -- If you later enable AI Insights sentiment, uncomment these and add the
//    scored column to Responses. See queries/03-textanalysis.m for the licence
//    and data-protection caveats before you do.
// Average Sentiment = AVERAGE ( Responses[SentimentScore] )
// Negative Answers  = CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( Responses ), Responses[SentimentScore] < 0.4 )


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Integrity  (reconciliation page)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Every one of these should read zero. They are on the report so that a data
// fault is stated rather than absorbed -- see BUILD.md 9.10 and 9.11.

// Preference rows for a role the person was not offered. Non-zero means
// Eligibility and Preferences disagree, which only Dataverse can settle.
Ineligible Preference Rows = COUNTROWS ( PreferenceIntegrity )

People With Ineligible Preferences = DISTINCTCOUNT ( PreferenceIntegrity[EmployeeID] )

// Preference rows discarded for having no row in People. A tester is expected;
// a colleague is not, and nothing else in the report would show them.
Orphaned Preference Rows = COUNTROWS ( OrphanedPreferences )

Orphaned Employee IDs = DISTINCTCOUNT ( OrphanedPreferences[IdAsStored] )

// People the what-if could place nowhere because none of what they ranked was
// theirs to rank. Kept apart from Unplaceable, which is a capacity answer.
People With No Eligible Options =
CALCULATE (
    DISTINCTCOUNT ( WhatIfAssignment[EmployeeID] ),
    WhatIfAssignment[EligibleRanked] = 0
)

// The gap between what people ranked and what the allocation could use. Zero
// when Eligibility and Preferences agree for everyone.
Ranked But Not Eligible =
SUMX ( WhatIfAssignment, WhatIfAssignment[RolesRanked] - WhatIfAssignment[EligibleRanked] )


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Eligibility-aware role filtering
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// How many of the CURRENTLY SELECTED people are eligible for this role.
//
// Put it on any visual whose rows come from DimRole, as a visual-level filter
// set to "is not blank". Without it a role dimension ignores every People
// slicer -- filters do not flow from People to DimRole, and a measure like
// Posts For Role returns a value for every role regardless, so nothing is ever
// pruned and an SG5 selection still lists Team Leader roles. See BUILD.md 9.14.
//
// It works because Eligibility is the many side of BOTH People and DimRole, so
// in a DimRole row under a People slicer it is filtered from both directions at
// once. No TREATAS needed: the row context does the work the relationships
// cannot do for DimRole itself.
People Eligible For Role = COUNTROWS ( Eligibility )

// Roles in the capacity sheet that nobody at all was offered, and the posts on
// them. These cannot be filled by this process. Reconciliation page only --
// leaving them in a subscription chart shows permanent undersubscription that
// no amount of interest could ever resolve.
Roles Offered To Nobody =
CALCULATE (
    COUNTROWS ( DimRole ),
    FILTER ( DimRole, ISEMPTY ( RELATEDTABLE ( Eligibility ) ) )
)

Posts On Roles Offered To Nobody =
CALCULATE (
    SUM ( DimRole[Posts] ),
    FILTER ( DimRole, ISEMPTY ( RELATEDTABLE ( Eligibility ) ) )
)


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Relationship bypass  (diagnostic pair -- see BUILD.md 9.16)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Same questions as Roles Offered To Selection and Posts Offered To Selection,
// but reaching Eligibility with TREATAS instead of through the People ->
// Eligibility relationship. They therefore return the right answer whether or
// not that relationship is working.
//
// PUT BOTH ON THE PAGE NEXT TO THE ORIGINALS. The comparison is the test:
//
//   bypass differs from the original  -> the relationship is the fault. The
//                                        original is reading every row in
//                                        Eligibility because nothing is
//                                        filtering it.
//   bypass agrees with the original   -> the relationship is fine and the
//                                        DATA gives those people those roles.
//                                        Check GradeReconciliation against the
//                                        workbooks.
//
// These are not the versions to ship. A measure that routes around a broken
// relationship hides it, and the next measure written against Eligibility
// breaks the same way with nothing to warn you. Fix the relationship, confirm
// the originals match these, then delete these two.
Roles Offered Bypass =
VAR Ppl = VALUES ( People[EmployeeID] )
RETURN
    CALCULATE (
        DISTINCTCOUNT ( Eligibility[RoleKey] ),
        TREATAS ( Ppl, Eligibility[EmployeeID] )
    )

Posts Offered Bypass =
VAR Ppl  = VALUES ( People[EmployeeID] )
VAR Keys =
    CALCULATETABLE (
        VALUES ( Eligibility[RoleKey] ),
        TREATAS ( Ppl, Eligibility[EmployeeID] )
    )
RETURN
    CALCULATE ( SUM ( DimRole[Posts] ), TREATAS ( Keys, DimRole[RoleKey] ) )
